<header><p class="st-kicker">emirate · Mesopotamia and Iran</p><h1 class="st-title">Hamdanid emirate of Mosul</h1><p class="st-meta">905 CE – 990 CE (approx.) · capital Mosul</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Arab emirate of the Taghlib family of Hamdan in northern Iraq, whose ruler Nasir al-Dawla briefly controlled the Abbasid caliph in Baghdad.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamdanid-dynasty">Hamdanids</a>, from the Arab <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banu-taghlib">tribe of Taghlib</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-mesopotamia">Jazira</a>, rose as Abbasid commanders. In 905 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abul-hayja-abdallah-ibn-hamdan">Abu'l-Hayja Abdallah ibn Hamdan</a> was appointed governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mosul">Mosul</a>. His son al-Hasan, given the title <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nasir-al-dawla">Nasir al-Dawla</a> in 942, turned the governorship into a hereditary <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">emirate</a> over Mosul and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diyar-rabia">Diyar Rabi'a</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nasir-al-dawla">Nasir al-Dawla</a> was briefly amir al-umara, effective master of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a>, in 942 to 943, and later fought the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/buyid-dynasty">Buyid dynasty</a> for control of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a>. He was known for heavy taxation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-mesopotamia">Jazira</a>'s farmland. His brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sayf-al-dawla">Sayf al-Dawla</a> founded the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/hamdanid-emirate-of-aleppo">Hamdanid Emirate of Aleppo</a>, famed for its court poets such as al-Mutanabbi. The family favoured Shia <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> while ruling a mixed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni</a> and Christian population.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nasir-al-dawla">Nasir al-Dawla</a> was deposed by his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abu-taghlib">Abu Taghlib</a> in 967. In 978 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adud-al-dawla">Adud al-Dawla</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/buyid-dynasty">Buyids</a> expelled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamdanid-dynasty">Hamdanids</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mosul">Mosul</a>; a brief return ended around 990, when the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/uqaylid-dynasty">Uqaylid dynasty</a> took over.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/hamdanid-emirate-of-mosul.jpg" alt="Hamdanid emirate of Mosul" width="320" height="201"><figcaption>Image: Ro4444 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hamdanids_955_AD.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>905 CE – 990 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>emirate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Mosul</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Abu'l-Hayja Abdallah, Nasir al-Dawla, Abu Taghlib</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Shia Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Arabic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/iraq">Iraq</a>, <a href="/countries/syria">Syria</a>, <a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Hamdanids of Mosul, Hamdanid dynasty</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=948&amp;focus=hamdanid-emirate-of-mosul" aria-label="Hamdanid emirate of Mosul on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5616 871 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Hamdanid emirate of Mosul lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5936 1127L6134 1224M5936 1127L6367 1352M5936 1127L6085 1123"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6134 1224h0M6367 1352h0M6085 1123h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5936 1127h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5936 1127h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its capital. <a href="/atlas?year=948&amp;focus=hamdanid-emirate-of-mosul">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
